{
"segments": [
{
"text": "Case Six.",
"start": 0.75,
"end": 2.17,
"pause": 0.6
},
{
"text": "The Closed Post Office.",
"start": 3.05,
"end": 4.29,
"pause": 1.4
},
{
"text": "It was Sunday, and Tidewhistle Cove was having a quiet morning. The church bells had rung, the gulls were sleepy, and even the post office had its blind down and its closed sign on the door, as it always does on a Sunday.",
"start": 6.01,
"end": 19.63,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Wenna Polglaze had spent the whole winter knitting a blanket for her new granddaughter. It was soft and pale blue, with a row of little white puffins marching across one end. On Saturday evening she wrapped it in brown paper, ready to post on Monday, and left it on the bench in her porch while she went to bed.",
"start": 19.63,
"end": 38.67,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "On Sunday morning, it was gone.",
"start": 39.73,
"end": 41.55,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Agnes Bell went with Wenna down the main street. Outside the Kettle and Gull they met Tamsin Trevelyan, who had been at church since ten, and Jago Penhallow, who had been baking since five in the morning with two helpers and had not left the bakery. Both were very sorry to hear about the blanket.",
"start": 42.75,
"end": 60.31,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Then they met Mr Garland, a visitor who was staying at the inn. Over his arm was a pale blue blanket with a row of little white puffins across one end.",
"start": 61.45,
"end": 70.81,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Wenna gasped.",
"start": 71.85,
"end": 72.43,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "\"Oh, this?\" said Mr Garland smoothly. \"I bought this at the post office this morning. They have a little craft counter, you know. Local knitting. Lovely, isn't it? I bought it not an hour ago.\"",
"start": 73.79,
"end": 85.45,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Wenna opened her mouth, but Agnes put a gentle hand on her arm. It was true that the post office did sell local knitting on its craft counter. Wenna herself had sold scarves there.",
"start": 86.65,
"end": 97.57,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "But Agnes was looking at the post office, down at the end of the street.",
"start": 98.49,
"end": 102.25,
"pause": 1.1
},
{
"text": "Can you solve it? How does Agnes know that Mr Garland is not telling the truth?",
"start": 103.51,
"end": 107.99,
"pause": 1.2
},
{
"text": "Think about it.",
"start": 109.43,
"end": 110.41,
"pause": 0.9
},
{
"text": "Take a moment, or pause here if you are listening. When you are ready, the solution follows.",
"start": 111.09,
"end": 116.25,
"pause": 4.5
},
{
"text": "The Solution.",
"start": 121.05,
"end": 121.63,
"pause": 1.1
},
{
"text": "Mr Garland took the blanket.",
"start": 122.89,
"end": 124.71,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "He said he had bought it at the post office that morning. But it was Sunday, and the post office was closed, with its blind down and its closed sign on the door, as it is every Sunday. Nobody could have bought anything there that morning.",
"start": 125.65,
"end": 139.59,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Tamsin had been in church and Jago had been in his bakery with two helpers. Mr Garland was the only one holding a blanket, and the only one with a story that could not be true.",
"start": 140.87,
"end": 151.65,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Mr Garland admitted he had seen the parcel on the porch bench on his early walk, and when a corner of the paper came loose and he saw the puffins, he simply couldn't help himself. He apologised to Wenna, who forgave him on the condition that he paid for the postage to send it to her granddaughter. He did, on Monday morning, when the post office was open.",
"start": 152.69,
"end": 173.39,
"pause": 0.0
}
],
